<h2>The problem the statute created</h2>
<p>The 2005 Act was drafted around a male respondent. Section 2(q) limited &ldquo;respondent&rdquo; to an adult male in a domestic relationship with the aggrieved person, and the proviso extended complaints by wives and female partners to &ldquo;a relative&rdquo; of the husband or partner. Litigation promptly exposed the seams. Could a widow complain against her mother-in-law alone? Could daughters proceed against a brother and his wife together, or did the sister-in-law escape because no complaint lay against a female except as an appendage to one against a male? The Bombay High Court, in the order under appeal in <em>Harsora</em>, had read the provision down: female relatives could be arrayed as co-respondents alongside an adult male, but not independently.</p>

<h2>What the Supreme Court held</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Before Harsora</strong><br>Respondent = &ldquo;adult male person&rdquo; in a domestic relationship; female relatives reachable, if at all, only through the proviso and only alongside a male respondent.</div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>After Harsora</strong><br>The words restricting the definition to adult males were struck down as violating Article 14; the proviso fell with them as redundant. A respondent is any person in a domestic relationship with the aggrieved person.</div>
</div>
<p>The Bench of Justices Kurian Joseph and R.F. Nariman tested the classification against the Act&rsquo;s own object — protecting women from violence occurring within the shared household — and found no rational connection between that object and a male-only respondent class. Violence within households is not a male monopoly; a definition that immunised female perpetrators defeated the statute it served. Applying the doctrine of severability, the Court deleted the offending words rather than the provision, leaving the rest of the Act intact.</p>

<h2>How respondent arrays are tested today</h2>
<p>The gatekeeping concept is now the domestic relationship under Section 2(f): persons who live or have lived together in a shared household, related by consanguinity, marriage, a relationship in the nature of marriage, adoption or joint family membership. Practically, three screens apply to every array:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Relationship screen.</strong> Does the complaint plead a domestic relationship with each named respondent — not merely kinship on paper, but the shared-household connection the definition contemplates?</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Allegation screen.</strong> Are there specific acts attributed to each respondent? Courts quash or discharge where distant relatives are roped in with omnibus allegations — a discipline imported from Section 498A practice.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Relief screen.</strong> Some reliefs have their own internal limits; the array should match the reliefs actually sought against each person.</div>
</div>

<h2>Strategic notes for both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>For complainants: plead the domestic relationship respondent-by-respondent, with periods of shared residence and specific incidents. Harsora removed the gender objection, not the pleading burden.</p>
  <p>For respondents: the attack has shifted from &ldquo;a woman cannot be arrayed&rdquo; to &ldquo;no domestic relationship or no specific allegation is pleaded against this respondent&rdquo; — a fact-based challenge, raised at the earliest stage.</p>
  <p>Remember the Act&rsquo;s complainant-side remains gendered: the aggrieved person must be a woman. Harsora widened whom she can proceed against, not who may complain.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer states the general legal position for information. The maintainability of any particular complaint depends on its pleadings and the relationships actually established.</div>
